perant matching in drosophila. biorxiv | 2022–05. 687,"Methods, theory & everything else","Tzuhsuan Ma, Rishika Mohanta, Glenn Turner, Ann Hermundstad, HHMI Janelia Research Campus, United States","To guide effective behavior in the face of uncertainty and change, animals can efficiently use their limited computational resources by adopting widely generalizable behavioral strategies. To gain a holistic understanding of the space of generalizable strategies, it is important to use a diversity of tasks to probe animal behavior. However, we lack principled methods for designing such tasks. Here, we combine enumeration with task design in order to probe the space of strategies that an animal uses to dynamically select among multiple options that can deliver different rewards. We consider a class of strategies in which an animal’s choices depend locally on the outcomes of previous actions. We then design and enumerate a class of tasks that best discriminate between individual strategies. Through this enumeration, we discover a set of highly discriminative tasks that would not have been devised by typical handcrafted methods. We show that these tasks can evoke and identify nearly all possible groupings of strategies, and can thus be combined to iteratively localize decision strategies from behavioral observations. Moving forward, we plan to use these tasks to uncover the cognitive architectures that guide decisions across different species—from flies to humans.",3.12959885597229,6.049147605895996,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paperbe81.html?PaperNum=1217,
1222,2023,Environmental motion presented ahead of self-motion modulates the heading direction estimation,Jongmin Moon,bayesian observer model | causal inference | multisensory integration | self-motion | perceptual bias,Decision-making and metacognition,"Jongmin Moon, Liana Saftari, Oh-Sang Kwon, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology, Korea (South)","The ability of a moving observer to accurately perceive their heading direction is essential for maintaining balance and effective locomotion. Previous research has identified mechanisms for integrating multisensory signals presented simultaneously, but it remains unclear how observers integrate visual signals collected before and during their own movement to perceive their heading direction. Here we explore the impact of environmental motion presented ahead of self-motion on heading perception. Human observers sat on a platform, viewed visual motion stimuli, and then indicated the direction of their movement after the platform had moved. Our results demonstrate that environmental motion presented before the observer’s movement significantly influenced their self-motion perception. We explain this result using an optimal causal inference model that takes into account the causal relationship between visual signals generated before and during observer movement. Overall, our study highlights the crucial role of environmental motion presented before self-motion in multisensory integration and self-motion perception.",4.139812469482422,7.682103633880615,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper217e.html?PaperNum=1222,
1613,2023,Evaluating the Role of Edge-Surface Reconstruction in Complex Lightness Illusions,Srijani Saha,visual perception | lightness illusion | deep learning | u-net | image reconstruction,Naturalistic encoding/decoding,"Srijani Saha, George Alvarez, Harvard University, United States","Lightness illusions demonstrate that how bright an object appears depends on an elaborate constructive process, to the point that the same surface can be perceived as either black or white depending on the context. Why does the biological visual system work this way? Our work examines the degree to which edge-surface reconstruction mechanisms can explain lightness illusions present in complex scenes (e.g., the moon illusion, Anderson & Winawer 2005). First, across two behavioral tasks, 56 participants matched the brightness of a disc presented in a dark or light hazy background to illustrate the effect of the context on lightness perception. Second, we trained a reconstructive U-Net model to output a filled-in image from edge-only inputs, thus computationally solving the same problem of the biological system: going from edges to a filled-in perceptual experience. Surprisingly, we found that, when reconstructing the discs, the U-Net model made systematic errors consistent with lightness illusions measured in people, suggesting a significant role of edge-surface reconstruction for this complex illusion.",7.965620994567871,7.095928192138672,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper1670.html?PaperNum=1613,
1126,2023,Evidence for macroscopic traveling waves from sEEG,David Alexander,macroscopic cortex | traveling waves | phase dynamics | seeg | intra-cranial,"Memory | Methods, theory & everything else | Decision-making and metacognition","David Alexander, Laura Dugué, Université Paris Cité, CNRS, France","Macroscopic traveling waves (MTWs) in the cortex (>15cm) have been associated with cognitive processes such as perception, attention and working memory, as well as revealing single-trial relationships to event-related potentials (ERPs). However, their status remains unresolved, with some studies suggesting they result from localized oscillatory sources and blurring artifact from extra-cranial measurements. We apply a novel method to estimate the spatial frequency (SF, i.e., the characteristic scales) of phase-gradients in sEEG (stereotactic electro-encephalography). In humans, these intra-cranial depth contacts offer good spatial resolution and often-times good spatial coverage of the cortex with minimal blurring artifact. We find the spectral power of phase-gradients is highest at the longest wavelengths, up to the size of the measurement array (40cm). This means that MTWs dominate activity in the cortex and suggests that M/EEG are suitable tools for their study since they primarily distort high (and not low) SF components. We interpret this large-scale coherent field as evidence for phase-gradients maintaining a distinct global organization.",6.36052942276001,4.107937812805176,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper438f.html?PaperNum=1126,
1341,2023,Evolving plastic networks that can learn novel cognitive tasks,Thomas Miconi,recurrent neural networks | neuromodulation | reinforcement learning | meta-learning | evolution overview we are evolving a plastic (that is | self-modifying) | fullyconnected recurrent network to be able to autonomously acquire novel simple cognitive tasks. importantly | we stress that the network is not just trained to perform a fixed set of tasks | but to acquire novel tasks | never seen before. during each trial | the network observes two stimuli in succession | produces a response | then receives a reward signal (which depends on the task | stimuli and response for this trial). concurrently | synaptic plasticity occurs at every synapse | under control of the network itself (as detailed below). the network is exposed to a block of 400 such trials of a given task | constituting the “lifetime” of the agent | during which it is expected that the network will learn the task through the operation of reward-modulated plasticity | as guided by its evolved innate weights and plasticity parameters. there is no task id: the agent must learn the task anew from stimuli and reward alone. figure 1: schema of the experiment the process described above is iterated (with a randomly chosen task from the training set for each pass) over a third | evolutionary loop | in which evolution optimizes the network’s structural parameters (innate weights w and plasticity parameters π - see below) in order to improve the network’s task learning ability (as estimated by the network’s performance over the last 100 trials of the task loop). we simplify the framework of y ang by only using binary stimuli and responses. the set of all possible tasks is thus the set of all 16 mappings from two successive binary stimuli to a binary response. at regular intervals | we test the network on a withheld “test” meta-task | never seen during evolution | to assess the network’s performance on the overall objective: automatic acquisition of unseen cognitive tasks. this withheld test task is “delayed match to sample” (dms: are the two successive stimuli identical?). lifetime plasticity is implemented by simple neuromodulated hebbian plasticity. conceptually | every connection maintains a hebbian “eligibility trace” | corresponding to a running average of the recent products of inputs and outputs at this connection. in parallel | at any point in time | the network produces a neuromodulatory signal m(t) | which determines how much of this eligibility trace is to be incorporated into the actual connection weights. more precisely | at any time | the fully-connected recurrent network acts according to the following equations: τ dx(t) dt = (w + π ⊙ p(t))r(t) − x(t) (1) r(t) =tanh(x(t)) (2) τh dh(t) dt = x(t)r(t)t − h(t) (3) dp(t) dt = ηm(t)h(t) (4) here x is the vector of neural activations (the linear product of inputs by weights) | r is the neural firing rates (activations passed through a nonlinearity) | w and π are the innate weights and plasticity parameters (the structural | “genetic” parameters | fixed and unchanging during a lifetime and optimized across lifetimes by evolution) | p is the plastic weights (initialized to 0 at the start of each lifrtime and changing over a lifetime according to the plasticity rule described in eqs. 3 and 4) and ⊙ represents the pointwise (hadamard) product of two matrices. m(t) response stimuli rewards figure 2: network organization 996figure 3: training loss over 1500 generations (left) and performance over a full block of 400 trials at generations 0 and 1500 (right). see text. figure 4: decoding of correct response for the (withheld) dms task. each data point represents performance in decoding correct response for the trial from neural firing rates at time 0 < x < 1000ms. the network does not reliably encode target response (“are the stimuli equal?”) during the response period at generation 0 (uniform 0 values in top-right matrix reflect complete saturation of network responses due to essentially random weight changes) | but does after evolution and lifetime learning (bottom-right plot). note that eqs. 1-2 are just the standard rnn equations | except that total weights include a plastic component π ⊙ p(t). h(t) is the hebbian eligibility trace (decaying running average of input-output product at each synapse). m(t) is the network-generated neuromodulation signal that gates the hebbian trace h(t) into actual plastic weights p(t) | while hebbian eligibility traces decay | actual plastic weights p(t) do not. note that tauh >> tau. importantly | plasticity is modulated by the network-generated signal m(t) | not by received rewards themselves: rewards are just an additional stimulus | with no direct impact on plasticity | but must be integrated with other information by the network to adequately guide plasticity through network-generated m(t). figure 5: dynamics of neuromodulation (orange line) and rewards (blue line) | over a full block of 400 trials (1500th generation | dms task). each blue spike is the reward received at the end of a trial. the x-axis indicates timesteps of 20ms each. relationship between received rewards and network-controlled neuromodulatory output changes over time. results figure 3 reports the evolutionary loss (that is | the mean correct rate over the last 100 trials of each successive lifetime) as a function of the number of generations for both training-set tasks (blue curve) and the withheld dms task (red curve | medians and inter-quartile ranges over 6 runs). learning performance on dms is poor at generation 0 | as expected for a randomly initialized network (figure 3 | right panel | top). by contrast | after evolution has sculpted the innate parameters of the network | the genotype now supports competent lifetime acquisition of the unseen cognitive task (figure 3 | bottom). performance increase is not simply due to a mere indiscriminate increase in overall plasticity: randomly swapping values of w or π reduces performance to chance level. how does the evolved network learn? we run a fully evolved network over 400 trials of the withheld dms task | while recording both the rewards received and the network-controlled neuromodulatory output m(t) (figure 5). m(t) approximates a reward prediction error signal | being mostly positive for high rewards and negative for low rewards. however | the precise mapping from rewards to m(t) turns out to change significantly over time. initially | m(t) produces only negative responses to low rewards | with little response to the few high rewards obtained by chance (early period | red frame). then | as high rewards become more frequent | m(t) responds both negatively to low rewards and positively to high rewards (middle period | green frame). finally | as learning becomes more reliable | m(t) slowly decays in magnitude (purple frame) | and mostly vanishes in the late trial | preventing further plasticity from disturbing the evidently successful learned connectivity. 997references y ang | g. r | joglekar | m. r | song | h. f | newsome | w. t | wang | x.-j. . task representations in neural networks trained to perform many cognitive tasks. nature neuroscience | 22(2) | 297–306. 998,"Methods, theory & everything else | Attention & cognitive control / executive function | Neural population geometry & dynamics | Naturalistic encoding/decoding","Thomas Miconi, ML Collective, United States","Animals can acquire cognitive tasks (i.e. tasks that require working memory) automatically, from stimuli and rewards alone, through the operation of their own evolved internal machinery. Can we harness this process to generate artificial agents with such abilities? Here we evolve neural networks, endowed with plastic connections and neuromodulation, over a set of simple cognitive tasks adapted from a popular framework. Importantly, the actual weight modification process (synaptic plasticity and neuromodulation) is entirely under control of the evolving network itself, rather than being guided by an external algorithm: the agent must evolve its own internal reinforcement learning system to acquire novel tasks. We show that the resulting evolved networks can automatically modify their own connectivity to acquire novel simple cognitive tasks, never seen during evolution, from stimuli and rewards alone, through the spontaneous operation of their evolved neural organization and plasticity system. Decoding analyses dissociate the effects of evolution and lifetime learning on emerging task representations. The evolved learning system turns out to implement an adaptive reward prediction error, switching through different reliably identifiable phases over time as task-learning progresses, responsive to ongoing performance.",5.005814075469971,6.37915563583374,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper749a.html?PaperNum=1341,
1638,2023,Excitatory-inhibitory cortical feedback enables hierarchical credit assignment,Will Greedy,hierarchical credit assignment | bursting | inhibitory interneurons | cortical microcircuits | deep learning | synaptic plasticity | biologically plausible learning | sensory discrimination,Neural population geometry & dynamics | Naturalistic encoding/decoding,"Will Greedy, Heng Wei Zhu, Joseph Pemberton, Kevin Nejad, Jack Mellor, Rui Ponte Costa, University of Bristol, United Kingdom","The error-backpropagation (backprop) algorithm remains the most common solution to the hierarchical credit assignment problem in artificial neural networks. In neuroscience, it remains unclear whether the brain adopts a similar strategy. Recent models have attempted to bridge this gap but they are either ineffective at propagating error signals through several brain areas or require multi-phase learning, neither of which are reminiscent of learning in the brain. Here, we introduce Bursting Cortico-Cortical Networks (BurstCCN), a model which solves these issues by integrating biologically-plausible bursting, dendritic feedback and cell-type specific functional connectivity. BurstCCN relies on apical dendrite-targeting (SST) interneurons to maintain E/I balance and facilitate the encoding of error signals. We demonstrate that the BurstCCN can effectively backpropagate errors through multiple areas using a single-phase learning process. Next, we show that the BurstCCN is capable of learning non-trivial sensory discrimination (MNIST and CIFAR-10) as well as a reinforcement learning task (Frozen Lake). Finally, we demonstrate how Dalean constraints can be introduced, proposing a role for multiple inhibitory cell-types. Overall, our work suggests that specific cortico-cortical connectivity with both short- and long-term plasticity, jointly underlie efficient hierarchical credit assignment in the brain.",5.212670803070068,7.219674587249756,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper2b0b.html?PaperNum=1638,
1368,2023,Explainable Deep Learning for Arm Classification During Deep Brain Stimulation - Towards Digital Biomarkers for Closed-Loop Stimulation,Mathias Ramm Haugland,deep brain stimulation | tremor | deep learning | cnn | digital biomarkers,"Methods, theory & everything else","Mathias Ramm Haugland, Anastasia Borovykh, Yen Tai, Shlomi Haar, Imperial College London, United Kingdom","Deep brain stimulation (DBS) is an effective technique for treating motor symptoms in neurological conditions like Parkinson’s disease and dystonic and essential tremor (DT and ET). The DBS delivery could be improved if reliable biomarkers could be found. We propose a deep learning (DL) framework based on EEGNet to search for digital biomarkers in EEG recordings for discriminating neural response from changes in DBS parameters. Here we present a proof-of-concept by distinguishing left and right arm movement in raw EEG recorded during a DBS programming session of a DT patient. Based on the classification of 1s segments from six-channel EEG, we achieve an average accuracy of up to 93.8%. In addition, we propose a simple, yet effective model-agnostic filtering strategy for explaining the network’s performance, showing which frequency band features it mostly uses to classify the EEG.",5.326216220855713,3.900329828262329,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper3907.html?PaperNum=1368,
1449,2023,Explaining the function of the N400 as the update of the internal representations of ANN models of sentence processing,Alessandro Lopopolo,n400 | semantic processing | recurrent neural networks | language models | sentence gestalt model | surprisal although the n400 has been extensively linked to language processing | there is still an ongoing debate surrounding its nature | specifically the function and the mechanisms that give rise to it. according to a study by rabovsky | hansen | and mcclelland | the internal dynamics of the sentence gestalt model (mcclelland | st. john | taraban | 1989) exhibit a similar pattern to the amplitude of the n400 under certain conditions known to modulate this event-related potential (erp) | suggesting that the n400 could reflect the update in an implicit predictive representation of meaning associated with semantic prediction errors. in the present study | we examine the update of the internal representations of two artificial neural network models in relation to the amplitude of the n400 measured during sentence comprehension (frank | otten | galli | vigliocco | 2015). while the models share a comparable recurrent architecture | they were trained on the same corpus data but on two distinct linguistic tasks: the sentence gestalt model (sgm) on predictive sentence comprehension | and the next-word prediction language model (lm) on predicting words in a sequence. we sought to determine whether the effect of the update of the sgm (su.sgm) on the n400 is dependent on the recursive integrative mechanism that underpins the model | or on the semantic function that the it approximates as a whole. additionally | we compared the effect of the sgm on the n400 to that of lexical surprisal | as per the hypothesis that the n400 signifies lexical prediction error (van petten | kutas | 1990 | hale | 2001 | parviz | johnson | brock | 2011 | frank | 2015 | michaelov | bergen | 2020). the models both the sgm and lm are based on similar recurrent architectures that process sentences sequentially and generate integrative representations at the recurrent layer. their architectures differ however with regard to their post recurrent layers | especially their output layers | reflecting the different functions that the models are trained to approximate. (a) sgm (b) lm figure 1: the sgm (a) and the lm (b). from an architectural point of view | the sgm differs from the lm for the,"Methods, theory & everything else | Language/auditory neuroscience | LLMs, reasoning, interpretability | Naturalistic encoding/decoding","Alessandro Lopopolo, Milena Rabovsky, University of Potsdam, Germany",We predict the amplitude of the N400 during naturalistic sentence processing by using the updates of the internal representations of a Sentence Gestalt Model and a next-word prediction Language Model trained on the same corpus. The findings showed that changes in N400 amplitude correspond to the update in the implicit predictive representation of meaning during sentence comprehension. The comparison between the Sentence Gestalt Model activity and lexical surprisal might indicate two distinct but closely related sub-processes contributing to sentence processing.,7.656209945678711,3.4789321422576904,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper877a.html?PaperNum=1449,
1692,2023,Exploring a Basis Set of Intrinsic Functions Underlying Neural Computation by Symbolically Programming Recurrent Neural Networks.,Daniel Calbick,intrinsic functions | neurobiology | programmable rnns | biologically-plausible rnns | multitask learning,"Methods, theory & everything else | Neural population geometry & dynamics","Daniel Calbick, Ilker Yildirim, Yale University, United States; Jason Kim, Cornell University, United States","Much like recurring cell types and circuit motifs, a basis set of functional primitives implemented in stereotyped neural dynamics can, in principle, be used to accomplish complex computation in the brain. Existing work using multitask-trained deep neural networks has established the emergence of functional modules in otherwise homogeneous networks. However, these approaches only allow for indirect exploration of the space of functional primitives due to the entanglement of training objectives, datasets, and architectures. Here, we take a direct, hypothesis-driven approach: inspired by classic work in computational neuroscience, we explore the basic building blocks of digital circuit functions, including oscillators, lag operators, and set-reset latches, as the basis set of functional primitives in the brain. Our approach is enabled by a new way of utilizing neural network computation. Instead of training, we program the weights and connectivity of RNNs to compute symbolically-specified functions. Using a standard multi-task learning battery, we show that these ""intrinsic function RNNs"" can support cognitive flexibility.",5.241539001464844,7.597573757171631,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper636e.html?PaperNum=1692,
1615,2023,Exploring Cognitive Factors in the Bayesian Pain Model: Precision Modulation and Mean Shifts,Andreas Strube,pain | bayesian pain model | placebo | nocebo the baysian pain model the concept of bayes-optimal processing in the brain has been used to explain placebo hypoalgesia and nocebo hyperalgesia | where positive and negative expectations respectively can affe ct pain perception (atlas | wager | 2014 | bingel | 2006 | colloca | benedetti | 2005 | petrovic | 2002 | 2004). these phenomena are thought to arise from the integration of top-down prior expectations with bottom up sensory signals in a bayesian pain model (büchel | 2014) . the integration can be approximated by gaussian distributions of the parameters | which all ows for an analytical solution (laplace approximation) to estimate the posterior distribution representing the pain percept. the posterior is dependent on the mean and precision of the prior and likelihood distributions | which can be described by two parameters | the mean and the precision of the distribution (μ and σ² | respectively). this formalization is expressed in the following equations | which represent the mean and precision of the resulting posterior distribution. (eq.1) μposterior = μprior ∗ σ² likelihood + μlikelihood ∗ σ² prior σ² likelihood + σ² prior σ2 posterior = σ2 likelihood ∗ σ2 prior σ2likelihood + σ2prior figure 1: the bayesian pain model. adapted from büchel . reproduced from strube . in figure 1 | gaussian distributions ( purple | y ellow) represent prior expectations (placebo | nocebo) and nociceptive sensory input (green) | which are integrated in a bayesian pain model to produce the pain percept represented by a posterior distribution ( blue). in example (a) | no prior expectations are generat ed | and the pain percept is identical to the nociceptive stimulus. in examples (b) and (c) | placebo and nocebo expectations are generated and integrated with the sensory input | resulting in a shift of the pain percept towards lower (placebo hypoalgesia) or higher (nocebo hyperalgesia) ratings on the visual analog scale (vas). 1057 this work is licensed under the creative commons attribution 3.0 unported license. to view a copy of this license | visit mean of the posterior distribution (red line) represents the estimated pain intensity. precision modulation and mean shifts the bayesian pain model proposes a framework for this integration by weighting somatosensory input with prior expectations based on their respective precision. however | it is crucial to consider how cognitive factors | such as attentional modulations or agency | can also influence pain perception within this model. the ability to incorporate cognitive factors in the bayesian pain model would allow for a more comprehensive understanding of pain perception. recently | we proposed a mo dulation of the mean or precision of the prior or likelihood distribut ions as a potential mechan ism through which cognitive factors influence pain perception in the bayesian pain model (strube | 2023) . the,Naturalistic encoding/decoding,"Andreas Strube, Christian Büchel, University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf, Germany","The Bayesian pain model proposes that pain perception arises from the integration of somatosensation (likelihood) and expectations (prior), which are weighted by their precision. However, the exact mechanisms by which cognitive factors influence pain perception within this model remain unclear. In this theoretical paper, we explore how cognitive factors can enter the Bayesian pain model via a precision modulation or mean shift.",4.03862190246582,4.1817121505737305,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper567d.html?PaperNum=1615,
1574,2023,Face-Off: Pitting Computational Models of the Fusiform Face Area Against Each Other with Controversial Face Stimuli,Wenxuan Guo,neural networks | representational geometry | representational similarity analysis | model comparison | controversial stimuli | fusiform face area,"Neural population geometry & dynamics | Methods, theory & everything else","Wenxuan Guo, Columbia University, United States; Tal Golan, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Israel; Heiko Schütt, Nikolaus Kriegeskorte, Columbia University, United States","Hundreds of studies have characterized the response properties of the fusiform face area (FFA), but we have yet to reveal the computational mechanisms underlying its representations. A methodological challenge is that distinct computational models can make indistinguishable predictions for randomly sampled faces. This fMRI study employs synthetic controversial face stimuli designed to elicit distinct predictions of six candidate neural network models of face representation in FFA. We present preliminary data from one participant scanned in four sessions. The controversial faces revealed many significant differences among the models in terms of their ability to predict FFA representational dissimilarity matrices (RDMs), whereas randomly sampled faces did not enable reliable adjudication among models. A neural network trained on inverse rendering—mapping face images to a latent space of a 3D face model—outperformed alternative models sharing the same architecture but trained on identification, classification, or autoencoding. Our results support the view that face recognition involves representations that reflect the physical structure of faces and demonstrate the need for optimized controversial stimuli to adjudicate among brain-computational models with neuroimaging experiments.",9.28279972076416,6.139372825622559,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper2411.html?PaperNum=1574,
1265,2023,Face-voice –integration in dorsal STS,Ilkka Muukkonen,face perception | naturalistic stimuli | sts | intersubject correlation | functional connectivity,Language/auditory neuroscience | Neural population geometry & dynamics | Naturalistic encoding/decoding,"Ilkka Muukkonen, Viljami Salmela, University of Helsinki, Finland","The superior temporal sulcus (STS) is involved in the processing of changeable aspects of faces as well as in auditive and multimodal processing. We measured fMRI while participants attended to naturalistic, emotional videos of people telling their life-stories in auditive, audiovisual and visual conditions. We used inter-subject correlation and functional connectivity to look how the audiovisual presentation of faces and voices modulates responses in the brain. We found a cluster in the dorsal STS responding more to audiovisual than visual or auditive stimuli. Connectivity between this and lateral occipital areas was heightened when audiovisual stimuli was present, and it seemed to process facial action units of the mouth region. Our results suggest that, specifically when speech is present, dorsal STS connects to the more posterior face processing areas and processes perceived mouth movements. We thus replicate and add up to earlier similar findings in a more naturalistic context with different analysis methods.",9.60727596282959,5.0912981033325195,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper4f11.html?PaperNum=1265,
1188,2023,Factorization of graphs in the compositional reuse of experience,Presentation Time: Sat,structure learning | memory | generalization | abstraction | factorization | compositionality a cognitive characteristic of humans is an ability to rapidly adapt to never experienced situations. a candidate mechanism supporting this is the compositional reuse of abstr acted elements underlying previous experiences. this form of compositionality may enable an arbitrary recombinat ion of prior knowledge in novel ways (barron | 2013 | behrens | 2018 | lake | 2015 | mark | 2020). e ven though everyday experiences present themselves as holistic scenes | they are invariably the product of multiple interac ting and temporally coincident sub-processes. for example | a picnic with friends in summer can be represented as a singular | holistic event | or by factorizing the sub-processes composing the scene – a group of people that can formally be described as a community graph structure and the current position in the seasonal cycle. while both state space representati ons afford meaningful predictions about structurally identical novel contexts | only the factorized representation facili tates generalization if new environmental subprocesses ov erlap those from past experiences. here | we tested the id ea that humans leverage factorized representations for such a compositional reuse of prior knowledge. we also det ail a candidate computational mechanism that allows for r apid adaption to never experienced situations. behavioral task and human behavior we presented healthy human volunteers ( n=238) with sequences of 12 compound images. each compound was a meaningful holistic scene | like a boy with a ball o r a dog on a blanket (figure 1a). unbeknown to participants | seq uences were generated by performing simultaneous traversal s on two graph factors and combining the associated images. for example | the sequence in panel figure 1a was a simu ltaneous traversal of a’->b’->c’… in the green graph factor and 7’->8’>9’… in the black graph factor. equivalently | sequences can be viewed as traversals on a single large compound graph. in a novel sequence learning task two groups of par ticipants first performed a prior learning phase (fig. 1b | le ft column). participants in condition 1 experienced sequences that were generated from a 4-state cyclic graph factor (deter ministic transitions) and a 6-state path-graph factor (proba bilistic transitions | fig. 1b | top row). participants in condition 2 saw compound image sequences produced by a 4-state path -graph factor (probabilistic transitions) and a 6-state cy clic graph factor (deterministic transitions | bottom row). the prior learning phase was followed by the transfer l earning phase | identical for both conditions (fig. 1b | righ t column). featuring 12 entirely new compound images composed from 10 new individual images. during transfer learning | the compound image sequence was generated by combining two cyclic graphs (deterministic transitions | 4 and 6 states). for both conditions | only one of the two experienced abstract graph factors was consistent across prior and transfer learning (e.g | the 4-state cycle in condition 1). since graph factors in both prior and transfer learning featured even numbers of nodes and a common multiple | the current position in both graph factor’s transition matrices stayed in sync throughout the respective task phase. therefore | during both task phases | participants only experienced a subset of 12 compound images and never saw the full set of all 24 possible image compositi ons figure 1. behavioral task. 946implied by the 4 x 6 graph factorization. the held- out compound stimuli in each task phase were then lever aged to assess knowledge of graph factorization. following each presented sequence | experience probes asked partici pants to predict upcoming states following one of the experi enced compound images (fig. 1c | left column). additionall y | inference probes (fig. 1c | right column) following each experience probe tested participants’ ability to infer and predict transitions between the held-out compounds. participants chose between the correct next compound image and a lure compound image. the lure compound image was matched with the correct compound image on one of the two individual images (e.g | d’8’ -> a’9’ or c’9’) | allowing us to test knowledg e of both graph factors separately. we reasoned that if subjects factorized and abstracted structure underlying their experienc e | they should show specifically enhanced performance for t he graph factor that was consistent across prior and transfer learning (e.g. 4-cycle for condition 1). we compared different hyp otheses about the data generating process by fitting a set of bayesian multilevel glms on prediction accuracy. the best-fitting glm featured an interaction effect for probe trial type (4-cycle vs 6cycle) and condition on probes about experienced an d inferred compositions (fig. 2a) (credible intervals around p arameter estimates > 0 | fig. 2b). transfer learning accuracy in the 4cycle was higher in condition 1 | while condition 2 showed increased 6-cycle performance. computational models and simulations we generated model predictions for human behavior under a range of different candidate learning mechanisms | i nvolving three successor feature models and a bayesian model | by simulating 250 agents presented with identical sequences as our participants. we considered a successor feature mod el ( m1 | stachenfeld | 2017) | learning an expected fut ure state occupancy from the sequence of unique product state s | and storing this quantity in the successor feature matrix ( m): |  | s‘) = |  | s‘) + ⍺  |  | ‘ – | ‘  α is the learning rate parameter | scaling the update of expected future state occupancy | γ is a discount parameter (0.9 in all simulations) | st denotes the current state | s’ denotes the next state in the sequence | is a one-hot vector (1 at position of st). since m1 does not factorize the experienced sequence | it cannot infer transitions between unobserved compoun ds and should perform at chance level in probes about infe rred compounds. additionally | due to absent factorization | m1 is not expected to show performance differences between th e 4-state and 6-state graph factor. we next considered a succ essor feature model ( m2 ) | learning expectations about the sequences of features in each graph factor separately (same u pdating mechanism as m1 | but operating over two separate feature sequences | for the 4-state and 6-state factor each) . m2 does factorize the experienced sequence and can successf ully infer transitions between unobserved compositions. for m1-2 | a softmax function generated choices from the value d ifference (vd ) of correct next state/features and incorrect alte rnative expectation for the current probe state | indicated by respective row entries in m. for m2 | we computed vd from the vector norms of the feature expectations of both choice op tions. the third successor feature model ( m3 ) was a hybrid between m1 and m2 | involving a trial-to-trial varying weighted avera ge of the degree to which expectations about upcoming states/features in m1 and m2 are used to generate choices. m1 ’s and m2 ’s softmax weights in predicting unobserved compositions (“model confidence”) were used to dete rmine trial-wise changes in model weighting. we did not p redict an increased accuracy for the graph factor consistent across prior and transfer learning for m1-3. this is because different images are used in prior and transfer learning (i.e. entir ely different sequences) and our successor feature models learn f rom experienced sequences without inferring the underly ing structure that produced the observations. m1-3 thus create separate ms for prior and transfer learning experience. final ly | we examined a bayesian model ( m4 | kemp and tenenbaum | 2008) performing trial-to-trial inferences over graph factors and a hypothesis space of graph structures. m4 updates a current hypothesis by comparing alternative hypotheses samp led from a prior. the final posterior over graph structures inferred in prior learning is used to update a prior “graph-gra mmar” to obtain a posterior serving as a prior for transfer learning. this reuse of knowledge facilitates faster discovery of similar structure. for example | encountering a 4-cycle stru cture increases the prior probability of 4-cycles | rendering them more likely to be considered subsequently as candidates. accuracy was obtained by comparing the current hypothesis to the correct sub-structures of the true underlying graph. m1-m3 did not recapitulate an increased accuracy for the graph factor consistent across prior and transfer learning (fig. 2c-e). instead | a bayesian model ( m4 ) captured humanlike transfer advantages (fig. 2f) | consistent with humans inferring factors underlying their experience and exploiting the attendant computational advantages of reusing these to facilitate learning and inference in new contexts. figure 2. behavioral results and computational model simulations. 947references barron | h. c | dolan | r. j | t. e. j. (2 013). online evaluation of novel choices by simultaneous representation of multiple memories. nature neuroscience | 16 (10) | 1492– 1498. behrens | t. e. j | muller | t. h | whittington | j. c. r | baram | a. b | k. l | kurth-nelson | z. . what is a cognitive map? organizing knowledge for flexible behavior. neuron | 100 (2) | 490–509. kemp | tenenbaum | j. b. . the discovery of structural form. proceedings of the national academy of sciences of the united states of america | 105 (31) | 10687– 10692. lake | b. m | salakhutdinov | j. b. . human-level concept learning through probabilistic program induction. science | 350 | 1332–1338. mark | moran | parr | kennerley | s. w | t. e. j. . transferring structural knowledge acr oss cognitive maps in humans and models. nature communications | 11 (1) | 1–12. stachenfeld | botvinick | m. m | gershman | s . j. . the hippocampus as a predictive map. nature neuroscience | 20 (11) | 1643–1653. 948,"Neural population geometry & dynamics | Naturalistic encoding/decoding | Methods, theory & everything else | Decision-making and metacognition","Presentation Time: Sat, 26 Aug, 13:00 - 15:00 United Kingdom Time","Real-world experiences are often the product of multiple dynamic sub-processes. Discovering these affords strong generalization in case of partial overlap or abstract resemblance between previous and current experience. Using a sequence learning task and computational modeling, we test whether humans (N=238) factorize experienced state spaces and subsequently reuse these in new contexts. In prior learning, we exposed some participants to sequences of compound images produced by the interaction of a 4-state cyclic graph and a 6-state path-graph, while another group experienced an interaction of a 4-state path-graph and a 6-state cyclic graph. During transfer learning, all participants experienced the product of a 4-state cyclic graph and a 6-state cyclic graph with new stimuli. Participants’ predictions about inferred unobserved transitions were used to assess knowledge of graph factors. We found that prediction accuracy in transfer learning for the 4-cycle graph was higher in the 4-cycle prior group and the 6-cycle graph prior group showed improved 6-cycle performance. Successor feature models did not explain this transfer effect. Instead, a Bayesian hypothesis testing model best captured the behavioral data. Our results support the idea that humans discover and decompose the factors generating their experience and reuse these sub-processes to generalize within new contexts.",3.8425140380859375,6.752373218536377,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper8358.html?PaperNum=1188,
1471,2023,Faster learning from slow features: The temporal coherence prior in human reinforcement learning,Noa Hedrich,reinforcement learning | representation learning | prior knowledge | temporal coherence | inductive bias,"RL, motor control & planning","Noa Hedrich, Sam Hall-McMaster, Max Planck Institute for Human Development, Germany; Eric Schulz, Max Planck Institute for Biological Cybernetics, Germany; Nicolas Schuck, Max Planck Institute for Human Development, Germany","Acquiring goal-directed behaviors requires us to learn which features of our environment are task-relevant, and which can be ignored. Machine learning research has suggested that meaningful information in the input data is often represented by features that change slowly over time, while fast variations may represent noise. Focusing on slowly changing features of the environment during learning could therefore be a useful bias for humans when selecting task-relevant features, even when the underlying task structure is unknown. To test this idea, we investigated whether humans are better at learning the reward predictiveness of slow vs fast changing features of two-dimensional bandits. We found that subjects accrued more reward during learning and achieved higher accuracy on subsequent test trials when a bandit's relevant feature changed slowly and its irrelevant feature fast, as compared to the opposite. Model fitting with a set of function approximation models that either had a single fixed learning rate or feature speed dependent learning rates showed that participants with a stronger effect adapted their learning rates to the feature coherence. These results provide evidence that human reinforcement learning is sensitive the timescales over which features change, akin to the ‘temporal coherence prior’ in the machine learning literature.",3.0616211891174316,5.769197940826416,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paperf3f2.html?PaperNum=1471,
1495,2023,Feature-disentangled reconstruction of perception from multi-unit recordings,Thirza Dado,feature disentanglement | generative modeling | neural coding | multi-unit activity,"Naturalistic encoding/decoding | Neural population geometry & dynamics | Methods, theory & everything else","Thirza Dado, Radboud University, Netherlands; Paolo Papale, Antonio Lozano, Netherlands Institute for Neuroscience, Netherlands; Lynn Le, Marcel van Gerven, Radboud University, Netherlands; Pieter Roelfsema, Netherlands Institute for Neuroscience, Netherlands; Yağmur Güçlütürk, Umut Güçlü, Radboud University, Netherlands","Here, we aimed to explain neural representations of perception, for which we analyzed the relationship between multi-unit activity (MUA) recorded from the primate brain and various feature representations of visual stimuli. Our encoding analysis revealed that the $w$-latent representations of feature-disentangled generative adversarial networks (GANs) were the most effective candidate for predicting neural responses to images. Importantly, the usage of synthesized yet photorealistic images allowed for superior control over these data as their underlying latent representations were known a priori rather than approximated post-hoc. As such, we leveraged this property in neural reconstruction of the perceived images. Taken together with the fact that the (unsupervised) generative models themselves were never optimized on neural data, these results highlight the importance of feature disentanglement and unsupervised training as driving factors in shaping neural representations.",7.946075439453125,5.9588189125061035,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper5a9d.html?PaperNum=1495,
1614,2023,Features and dynamics of social inference and decision-making in naturalistic human interaction,Nina Rouhani,social cognition | decision-making | emotion | naturalistic interaction,Social cognition & theory of mind | Decision-making and metacognition,"Nina Rouhani, Ralph Adolphs, John O'Doherty, Tessa Rusch, California Institute of Technology, United States","The ability to predict the behavior of others is a crucial aspect of adaptive learning. Here, we characterized the features and dynamics of social inference and action as pairs of participants got to know each other through online chatting, followed by an iterative decision-making game where they competed or collaborated with each other for monetary rewards. We developed a data-driven measure of social inference and found that chat and game interactions informed these inferences and predicted their changes. Moreover, to characterize the underlying mechanisms supporting social behavior, we developed and fit computational models of decision-making, integrating and relating them to the social inferences from our task. While we found a strong positivity bias when initially evaluating partners, this bias was reduced after the game. A computational model that combined individual inequity aversion with learning about a partner’s cooperation probability best fit the data. Model-generated estimates further predicted the decreases in positive trait ratings and increases in negative trait ratings that occurred after the game.",2.0366435050964355,6.009055137634277,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper220e.html?PaperNum=1614,
1639,2023,Feedforward Neural Networks can capture Human-like Perceptual and Behavioral Signatures of Contour Integration,Fenil R. Doshi,perceptual grouping | gestalt vision | psychophysics | deep neural networks methods and results the primary question of this work is whether feedforward architectures can show human-like sensitivities to contour integration. behavioral experiment to first address this question | we conducted a behavioral study with human participants using a similar experimental setup as field . we recruited 46 participants via prolific and presented them with a subset of 1000 synthetic images of gabor elements (see figure 1a). each image was comprised of an array of 256 gabor elements | among which 12 elements form an extended contour | while the remaining 244 elements are positioned randomly without any density cue to segregate the contour elements from the,"Methods, theory & everything else","Fenil R. Doshi, Talia Konkle, George A. Alvarez, Harvard University, United States","Contour integration is the process of linking local edge elements to arrive at a unified perceptual representation of a complete contour, and may thus serve as a critical pre-cursor representation needed to extract global shape information supporting object recognition. Many mechanisms have been proposed for such a feature-linking process (Field, Hayes & Hess, 1993; Kellman & Shipley, 1991), including long-range lateral interactions (Bosking et al., 1997), temporally synchronized cortical oscillations (Engel, Konig & Singer, 1991), and top-down feedback connections (Kim et al., 2019). In this study, we test the alternative possibility that feed-forward, nonlinear convolutional neural networks are able to perform contour integration without lateral connections, recurrence, or top-down feedback. We find that such a feedforward system exhibits sensitivities to global and local contour curvatures comparable to humans, but it requires two critical inductive biases to do so - visual experience of relatively straight-looking smooth contours and an architectural constraint of increasing receptive field size. Through this approach, we provide computational support for the hypothesis that a hierarchical feedforward visual processor can develop and leverage Gestalt-like laws of ""good continuation"" to detect extended contours in a manner consistent with human perception.",7.369372844696045,7.1072773933410645,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper4746.html?PaperNum=1639,
1048,2023,First Steps in Using Topographic Deep Artificial Neural Network Models to Generate Hypotheses about Not-yet-detected Functional Neural Clusters in the Ventral Stream,Kamila M. Jozwik,object vision | inferior temporal cortex | topography | selectivity | dimensionality | deep artificial neural networks,"Neural population geometry & dynamics | Naturalistic encoding/decoding | Methods, theory & everything else","Kamila M. Jozwik, Massachusetts Institute of Technology and University of Cambridge, United Kingdom; Hyodong Lee, Nancy Kanwisher, James J. DiCarlo, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, United States","Although several types of spatially-aggregated neural functional selectivities have been reported in the inferior temporal (IT) cortex of humans and monkeys, such as face, place, and body selectivities, broad swaths of IT have yet to be similarly characterized. Here, we present the first steps of using Topographic Deep Artificial Neural Networks (TDANNs) as hypothesis generators of not-yet-detected spatially-aggregated IT functional selectivities. To isolate the shared selectivities across a population of TDANNs, we applied hyperalignment to the IT layer of ten TDANNs. We then analyzed the shared underlying functional representations to identify eleven predicted neuronal functional selectivity clusters. After mapping these clusters back to the spatial IT maps in each TDANN, we find that face-selective units -- which spatially aggregate in TDANNs -- are strongly loaded on one of these functional clusters. On visual inspection, the other functional clusters appear to be selective for scenes, animal bodies, and mid-level object properties. Topographic ANNs, when analyzed in this manner, could be used to predict novel spatially-aggregated selectivities shared by all brains and to predict the spatial relationships between those functional aggregates. Both types of predictions could then be tested via targeted fMRI experiments.",8.944408416748047,6.825703144073486,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper8e56.html?PaperNum=1048,
1511,2023,Fluctuating responses evoked by multiple objects: a general feature of visual representations,Presentation Time: Fri,neural representation | neural code | multiplexing | object vision | figure ground segregation,Naturalistic encoding/decoding,"Presentation Time: Fri, 25 Aug, 15:00 - 15:15 United Kingdom Time","How neural representations preserve information about multiple stimuli is mysterious because tuning of individual neurons is coarse and the populations of neurons tasked with encoding each individual stimulus must in principle overlap. Here we show that when two perceptually distinguishable stimuli are presented, a subpopulation of neurons in MT and the IT face patch system exhibit fluctuating firing patterns, as if they responded to only one individual stimulus at a time. Furthermore, consistent with our previous results in the early visual area V1, the fluctuations in MT were only observed when the two stimuli formed distinguishable objects but not when they fused into one perceived object. In the face patches, fluctuations were observed for both face-face and face-object stimulus combinations. Taken together, these findings point toward an updated model of how the brain preserves sensory information about multiple stimuli for subsequent processing and behavioral action.",8.907183647155762,6.388577461242676,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper136e.html?PaperNum=1511&talk,
1228,2023,Fluctuations in Risk Attitudes Arise Systematically from Varying Noise in Bayesian Magnitude Perception,Gilles de Hollander,bayesian perception | neuroeconomics | risky decision-making,"Decision-making and metacognition | Naturalistic encoding/decoding | Methods, theory & everything else","Gilles de Hollander, Marcus Grueschow, Christian Ruff, University of Zurich, Switzerland","Humans are generally risk averse: They prefer options with low uncertainty over options with high uncertainty, even when the expected payoff is lower. Traditional economic theory explains risk aversion with diminishing utility of larger monetary amounts. More recent theories propose that risk aversion reflects a perceptual bias, where large payoffs are underestimated rather than undervalued due to regression towards a prior mean. These theories have some empirical support in work on individual differences: A person’s risk aversion indeed relates to their individual acuity of perceptual magnitude judgements. Here, we show that moment-to-moment fluctuations in risk attitudes reflect temporal fluctuations in the precision of payoff representations. We propose and validate a model that can account for empirically observed order effects in risky choices as the result of varying noise and Bayesian inference. Moreover, we show using Bayesian decoding techniques and 3T and 7T fMRI that on trials with neurally less acute number representations, choices are less consistent and more biased towards the subject’s individual prior, leading to either more risk-averse or more risk-seeking behavior. Our results have profound implications for economic theory, as they reveal how individual preferences are mechanistically influenced by random fluctuations in brain state and prior beliefs.",2.186677932739258,4.6558613777160645,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper381b.html?PaperNum=1228,
1472,2023,"Forgone, but not forgotten: separate episodic memories underlie explicit reports and eye movements",Yul Kang,episodic memory | ideal observer | bayesian modeling | causal inference | eye movements eye movements have been suggested to provide a unique window into memory processes that evade instructed | or explicit | reports (ryan | shen | 2020 | lancry-dayan | benshakhar | pertzov | 2022). here | building on research on “retrieval-dependent eye movements” (rdes | richardson | spivey | 2000 | johansson | holsanova | dewhurst | holmqvist | 2012 | staudte | altmann | 2017) | we examined explicit memory reports and eye movements to study the representations and mechanisms underlying episodic memory retrieval. we used a location memory task to compare how rdes are affected by a memorized object location and a subsequent suggestion. in the “learning phase” | participants were presented with a series of object-location pairs on a circular array and were asked to remember the location of each object for a later memory test (“l”earned location | figure 1a). next | in the “suggestion phase” | participants were again presented with a series of (50% correct) object-location pairs (“ s”uggested location | allegedly produced by another participant in the upcoming memory test) and were asked to make a binary judgment about the correctness of each pair (“ d”ecision). finally | in the “response phase” | participants were sequentially presented with a subset of the objects from the learning phase at the center of the screen. for each of these objects | participants were asked to indicate its original location on the circular array (“r”esponed location). explicit reports to understand the mechanisms determining the explicit reports given two sources of information ( l | where s is the same as l or a random different location | depending on whether it is “c”orrect | i.e. c = 1 or 0) | we fit and compared two models that perform causal inference about whether these two sources of information derive from the same cause (shams | beierholm | 2010). first | the optimal cue combination model (figure 1b without dark red arrow) | optimally combines noisy memories ˜lr and ˜sr of l and s to produce the reports d (reflecting inferences aboutc) and r (reflecting inferences about l) by performing bayesian inference (k ¨ording | 2007). second | in the conditioned memory model (figure 1b with dark red arrow) | d is produced as in the optimal model but (in analogy to models of conditioned perception | stocker | simoncelli | 2008) r is determined considering only the possibility that the judgment d was veridical (i.e | conditioning on c = d | dark red arrow from d to r). we used statistically principled maximum-likelihood to fit both memory models to the trial-by-trial reports ( r | d) of individual participants given the stimuli ( l | and compared the two models with bayesian information criterion (bic). using synthetic data | we extensively validated our model fitting and comparison approach | and confirmed that the models were identifiable (see e.g | figure 1e | left). both the optimal and the conditioned memory models gave comparable fits to the probability of judging the suggestion correct (figure 1c | row 1). however | their predictions about the pattern of responded locations differed markedly (figure 1c | row 2). the optimal model (figure 1c | column 2) predicts that when the suggestion is deemed correct (orange line) | the average report falls between the suggested (diagonal line) and the learned locations (horizontal line) | and the learned location is followed more closely when the suggested location is farther away from the learned location (figure 1c | row 3 | column 2 | orange line tends downward towards the extremes). this is because the optimal model weights the hypothesis that the suggestion comes from the same source as the learned location by its probability | which is lower when | s − l | is large. in contrast | the conditioned memory model predicts that the report follows the suggestion when it is deemed correct | and even more so when the suggested location is farther away from the learned location (figure 1c | column 3: orange line tends upward towards the extremes) | which matches the data better (orange markers | reproduced from column 1). bayesian model comparison provided overwhelming support for the conditioned memory model (figure 1e | row 1 | ∆bic > 10). thus | these results suggest that participants first performed causal inference based on memories ˜lr | ˜sr | and made the explicit report r ignoring the possibility that their decision d might be wrong. hence | from explicit reports alone | it appears as though the memory of s | once forgone | is forgotten. 4711 retrievallearning suggestion b c correct sugg. ( )s=l incorrect sugg. ( )s/uni2260l deem to be correct ( ) / incorrect ( ) s d=1 d=0 learned location ()l target image (2 s) blank (0.5 s) empty array (2 s | gazes between 0.5-1 s) g : responser a optim. no cond. yes hand only no no no optim. yes no yes cond. yes yes yes optim. yes no no cond. yes yes no ˜pg ls/uni2192gd/uni2192˜pr l d/uni2192˜pg l | s˜lr | ˜sr=˜lg | ˜sg ( rejected) d=0 s 0.5 time (s) 0 d ( accepted) d=1 s l r s l r=s 0 1 p(d=1 | s°l) data optimalconditioned -5 0 5r°le[r°l | s°l | d] d10 -5 0 5r°lp(r°l | d= 1) -5 0 5r°lp(r°l | d= 0) -5 0 5 s°l 0 1 r°ls°le[r°l | d] s°l -5 0 5 s°l -5 0 5 s°l 0.0 0.5 1.0 subject 11 data hand only full p(g-r | l-r | d=1) p(g-r | d=0) p(g-r | s-r | d=0) cond. same mem. gaze l−r s−r diﬀ. mem. g−r 0 1 p(d=1 | d] s°l -5 0 5 s°l -5 0 5 s°l 0.0 0.5 1.0 subject 11 0 1 p(d=1 | d] s°l -5 0 5 s°l -5 0 5 s°l 0.0 0.5 1.0 subject 11 same / different memories for and r g memories affect g conditioned memory for r conditioned memory for g c l s ˜lr r ˜sr gf d ˜lg ˜sg pr l | c(/uni22c5) ˜pr l(/uni22c5) pg l | s(/uni22c5) ˜pg l | s(/uni22c5) f=1…f t=1...tf wf | t e true model p(d=1 | s-l) e(r-l | s-l | d) / (s-l) data optim. cond. s−l 0 1 p(d=1 | s°l) data optimal conditioned -5 0 5r°le[r°l | d] d 1 0 -5 0 5r°lp(r°l | d= 0) -5 0 5 s°l 0 1 r°l s°le[r°l | d] s°l -5 0 5 s°l -5 0 5 s°l 0.0 0.5 1.0 subject 11 s−l r−l s−l 0 1 p(d=1 | d] s°l -5 0 5 s°l -5 0 5 s°l 0.0 0.5 1.0 subject 11 e(r-l | d) r−l p(d=1) diﬀ. mem. handgaze same mem. full hand diﬀ. mem. gaze same mem. full hand only optim. cond. optim. cond. optim. cond. winner model /uni22ef/uni22ef /uni22ef figure 1. a . behavioral task. b. generative model of hand (r) and gaze responses (gf ). the suggestion ( s) is either correct (c =1) such that it matches the learned location (s=l) or wrong (c =0 →s ̸=l). the memories of l and s are corrupted by noise | and are shared or distinct across the pathways controlling r and g (˜l/ ˜sr/g | blue lines/arrows). based on these memories | the observer infers the posterior distributions of l | and c (pr/g). after the decision about the suggestion ( d) | these posteriors may be conditioned on the assumption thatc =d (˜pr/g | red/orange arrows). r is generated based on ˜pr | and each fixation gf is generated based on r | and possibly also ˜pg (pink arrow). gaze locations wf | t corresponding to the same fixation f are sampled from a gf -specific 2d gaussian (one of 12 peripheral and 2 central gray open ellipses in d). purple: known to the experimenter | gray: known to the participant | green: known to both. arrows/lines with color: present only in a subset of models (see table). c. explicit reports of an example participant and model fits. data (column 1 | markers) and the fit of the optimal (column 2 | curves) and the conditioned memory models (column 3 | curves) to the data. models are fit to the full distribution of reports | mean is shown for visualization only. t op:probability of deeming the suggestion correct. given s−l. middle: circular mean of r−l given s−l | when s was deemed correct vs. wrong (d =0 /1 | blue/orange). dotted lines indicate r = s (diagonal) and r = l (horizontal). bottom: same as middle row | but normalized by s−l (undefined when s = l). dotted lines indicate r = s (top) and r = l (horizontal). d. example trials of the same participant when d = 0/1 (left/right). color indicates within-trial time | gray dots are gazes from other trials | ellipses are identified clusters. e. model recovery | comparison. top and bottom rows show hand and eye models | respectively. left: model recovery p(true model | winner model). middle: total bic of each model relative to the winner model. right: number of participants for whom each model was the winner. implicit responses we then analyzed eye movements during the response phase to determine whether they reveal memories aboutl and s beyond what is already conveyed by explicit hand reports. we first constructed two classes of models to analyze eye movements gaze-by-gaze (figure 1b). either class assumes that gaze locations form 14 clusters (figure 1d | open ellipses) | and that each gaze location ( w) is sampled from a 2d gaussian distribution associated with one of the clusters ( g). the two models differ only in what determines g. the “hand only” model (figure 1b without pink arrow) assumes that the explicit report r determines which cluster ( g) the gaze is assigned to. in contrast | the “full” model (figure 1b with pink arrow) assumes that both the explicit reports and the memory of l and s affect the gazes. consistent with the full model | we found trials where the gaze was clearly directed to s even when it was rejected | and to l instead of s even when s was accepted (figure 1d). fitting the models revealed that gaze locations (their cluster assignment | after subtracting the influence of r | were biased toward the direction of l and s | consistent with the full model | but not with the hand-only model (not shown). bayesian model comparison decisively supported the full gaze model (figure 1e | 1st versus all other models). we further discriminated between 2 ×2 variants of the full model | depending on whether they performed optimal cue combination or conditioned memory (figure 1b | orange arrow) | and whether the effects of l | s on eye-movements manifested via the same or different memories as that underlying explicit recall (figure 1b | blue lines vs. arrows). we found overwhelming evidence for separate memories underlying eye movements with optimal cue combination (figure 1e | penultimate model). therefore | our results demonstrate that there is a separate memory of s that is not forgotten even when it is explicitly forgone. 472references johansson | k. . eye movements during scene recollection have a functional role | but they are not reinstatements of those produced during encoding. journal of experimental psychology: human perception and performance | 1289. k¨ording | k. p | w. j | quartz | tenenbaum | j. b | shams | l. . causal inference in multisensory perception. plos one | 2(9) | e943. lancry-dayan | o. c | ben-shakhar | y . . the promise of eye-tracking in the detection of concealed memories. t rends in cognitive sciences . richardson | d. c | m. j. . representation | space and hollywood squares: looking at things that aren’t there anymore. cognition | 269–295. ryan | j. d | k. . the eyes are a window into memory. current opinion in behavioral sciences | 1–6. shams | u. r. . causal inference in perception. t rends in cognitive sciences | 14(9) | 425–432. staudte | g. t. . recalling what was where when seeing nothing there. psychonomic bulletin | review | 400–407. stocker | a. a | p . . a bayesian model of conditioned perception. in j. c. platt | d. koller | y . singer | s. t. roweis (eds.) | ( –1416). howard hughes medical institute. 473,Memory | Decision-making and metacognition,"Yul Kang, Queen Mary University of London, United Kingdom; Johannes Mahr, Harvard University, United States; Márton Nagy, Krisztina Andrási, Eötvös Loránd University, Hungary; Gergely Csibra, Central European University, Austria; Máté Lengyel, University of Cambridge, United Kingdom","Episodic memories are thought to integrate multiple aspects of a past experience into a unified engram. A key prediction is that such integrated memories should drive behavior consistently across different response modalities (e.g., explicit/implicit). This however, has remained largely untested, as previous approaches couldn’t resolve the content of memories underlying implicit responses. Here we used ideal observer-based trial-by-trial/gaze-by-gaze analyses of explicit reports and spontaneous eye movements to reconstruct participants’ memories underlying different response modalities. We used a false memory paradigm, where human participants studied a sequence of object-location pairings, followed by suggestions (50% false). After participants judged the correctness of all suggestions, they recalled each object’s location while their gazes were recorded. Our analyses revealed that explicit recall reflected ‘conditioned memory’: the recalled location reflected only either the suggested or the original location, depending on whether the suggestion had been deemed correct. In contrast, eye movements did not show this effect, and reflected a combination of memories distinct from those underlying explicit recall. Challenging the notion of unified engrams, these results suggest the formation of multiple distinct memory traces of the same experience that are maintained independently, show different vulnerability to false suggestions, and control different response modalities.",4.557159423828125,5.196919918060303,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paperd1c6.html?PaperNum=1472,
1556,2023,Frequency-Specific Contributions to Perceptual Priors in Audition: Testing the Predictive-Coding Prediction,Aviel Sulem,pitch | perceptual bias | low/high processing levels | top-down | bottom-up | predictive coding,"Methods, theory & everything else","Aviel Sulem, Itay Lieder, Merav Ahissar, Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Israel","Perceptual priors refer to the effect of previous experiences on our perception. They were shown to have both high- and low-level contributions, yet their interaction is still unknown. Specifically, whether bias favoring recently experienced low-level features results from previous high-level perception, in-line with predictive coding, or alternatively – it reflects low-level, bottom-up driven memory component. To decipher that, we used the “missing fundamental illusion”, a well-studied phenomenon in auditory perception, that allows a dissociation between low-level frequency components and high-level pitch perception. Surprisingly, we found that low-level effects are determined by low-level memory components. Namely, when the fundamental was missing, there was no contraction to the high-level pitch, but rather to the physically present frequency!",8.054060935974121,5.546701431274414,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper0514.html?PaperNum=1556,
1452,2023,From Primates to Robots: Emerging Oscillatory Latent-Space Dynamics for Sensorimotor Control,Alexander Mitchell,representation learning | machine learning | robotics | sensorimotor control,Neural population geometry & dynamics,"Alexander Mitchell, Oiwi Parker Jones, Jun Yamada, Wolfgang Merkt, Ioannis Havoutis, Ingmar Posner, University of Oxford, United Kingdom","Oscillatory signals drive reaching and locomotion for both robots and primates. Recent results in neuroscience have shown that periodic signals are present in the motor cortex of primates during rhythmic tasks such as locomotion as well as during linear movements notably reaching. Our works on learning latent motion representations in robotics revealed that oscillatory latent dynamics emerge automatically from training data for quadruped locomotion as well as manipulation tasks. Inspired by these works, we recreate the locomotion latent-spaces as well as create manipulation specific versions. With the latter we show that manipulation problems can be solved using periodic signals in a suitable latent-space. We see that these trajectories are reminiscent of those seen in the motor cortex. We artificially lesion the decoder of the locomotion model to understand how the correlations are captured. This results in deformation of both the oscillatory signals in the latent space and degradation in the locomotion trajectories.",5.997159481048584,6.044482231140137,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper4e4e.html?PaperNum=1452,
1683,2023,Functional Connectivity: Continuous-Time Latent Factor Models for Neural Spike Trains,Meixi Chen,multivariate point processes | spike trains | latent factor models | functional connectivity | neural correlation | classical conditioning,Neural population geometry & dynamics,"Meixi Chen, Martin Lysy, University of Waterloo, Canada; David Moorman, University of Massachusetts Amherst, United States; Reza Ramezan, University of Waterloo, Canada","Modelling the dynamics of interactions in a neuronal ensemble is an important problem in functional connectivity research. One popular framework is latent factor models (LFMs), which have achieved notable success in decoding neuronal population dynamics. However, most LFMs are specified in discrete time, where the choice of bin size significantly impacts inference results. In this work, we present what is, to the best of our knowledge, the first continuous-time multivariate spike train LFM for studying neuronal interactions and functional connectivity. We present an efficient parameter inference algorithm for our biologically justifiable model which (1) scales linearly in the number of simultaneously recorded neurons and (2) bypasses time binning and related issues. Simulation studies show that parameter estimation using the proposed model is highly accurate. Applying our LFM to experimental data from a classical conditioning study on the prefrontal cortex in rats, we found that coordinated neuronal activities are affected by (1) the onset of the cue for reward delivery, and (2) the sub-region within the frontal cortex (OFC/mPFC). These findings shed new light on our understanding of cue and outcome value encoding.",5.925308704376221,6.551645755767822,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper24de.html?PaperNum=1683,
1612,2023,Functional Double-Dissociation Along the Human Hippocampal Long Axis,Peter A. Angeli,hippocampus | hippocampal long axis | scene construction | space | salience,Neural population geometry & dynamics,"Peter A. Angeli, Lauren M. DiNicola, Noam Saadon-Grosman, Harvard University, United States; Randy L. Buckner, Harvard University; Massachusetts General Hospital, United States","The hippocampus has long been appreciated to exhibit specialization along its long axis. Recently, a within-individual fMRI approach which leverages data from densely sampled participants identified preferential functional connectivity from the anterior and posterior hippocampus to cerebral networks DN-A and SAL / PMN, respectively. Building on this work, we explored the functional responses of these hippocampal regions in two independent cohorts of extensively scanned individuals (n = 9 and n = 11). We replicated the differential functional connectivity in both data sets and characterized a functional double dissociation between the anterior and posterior hippocampus. The anterior region was sensitive to the extent that participants engaged in scene construction, while the posterior region showed transient activation in response to salient stimuli. These findings support the important role of the anterior hippocampus in scene processing and suggest a role for the posterior hippocampus in processing salient stimuli.",7.918406963348389,4.29619026184082,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper84e2.html?PaperNum=1612,
1370,2023,Functional relevance alters the neural geometry of novel instructed actions,Carlos González-García,cognitive control | working memory | neural geometry | rsa | mvpa,"Neural population geometry & dynamics | Memory | Naturalistic encoding/decoding | Methods, theory & everything else | Attention & cognitive control / executive function","Carlos González-García, University of Granada, Spain; Silvia Formica, Humboldt Universität zu Berlin, Germany; Ana F. Palenciano, University of Granada, Spain; Marcel Brass, Humboldt Universität zu Berlin, Germany","Different brain regions contain relevant information about upcoming novel instructed actions. In visual working memory, memories that are currently vs. prospectively relevant can be represented in orthogonal and even inverted patterns of activity. However, it remains unknown whether the state of priority of WM-guided novel actions modulates their representational format in a similar way. To explore this, we used fMRI while participants encoded two novel S-R mappings at the beginning of each trial. A cue later indicated whether one of the encoded mappings would be relevant in the first probe (“current” condition), in the second probe after having to answer to an irrelevant stimulus (“prospective” condition), or in the second probe after a free delay (“delayed” condition). We first confirmed that WM contents in current, prospective, and delayed conditions were decodable in relevant brain areas. However, we observed a lack of generalization between the neural codes of different conditions. Finally, model-based RSA revealed that planned actions of different priority are separated and rotated in space. These results suggest that the priority state of WM-guided novel actions impacts their representation in key brain regions and provide initial evidence of a divergence in the effects of priority in visual vs. action-oriented memories.",5.427011966705322,5.299659729003906,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paperdc93.html?PaperNum=1370,
1321,2023,Gamma-band sensory stimulation enhances episodic memory retrieval,Benjamin J. Griffiths,gamma oscillations | episodic memory | sensory stimulation | pyramidal-interneuronal network gamma,Memory | Neural population geometry & dynamics,"Benjamin J. Griffiths, University of Birmingham, United Kingdom; Daniel Weinert, Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität, Germany; Ole Jensen, University of Birmingham, United Kingdom; Tobias Staudigl, Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität, Germany","While transient increases in gamma-band activity have long been associated with successful memory retrieval, a causal relationship has yet to be established. Here, we address this by delivering high-frequency visual sensory stimulation during an associative memory task. We found that 32.5Hz and 65Hz visual sensory stimulation applied during recall led to an improvement in memory performance relative to baseline and control stimulation conditions. To understand the mechanistic underpinnings of this effect, we built a pyramidal-interneuronal network gamma (PING) model and drove the network with a variety of exogenous rhythms that emulate our sensory stimulation protocol. These analyses revealed that the aforementioned behavioral results are best explained by 32.5Hz and 65Hz sensory stimulation entraining a slower (i.e., 32.5Hz) gamma oscillation. Altogether, this suggests that gamma-band sensory stimulation can enhance recall by harmonically entraining an endogenous slow gamma oscillation.",5.157857418060303,4.905987739562988,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_papera931-2.html?PaperNum=1321,
1482,2023,Gazed and confused: Gaze reinstatement reveals effects of semantization,Rebekka Heinen,memory | representational format | deep neural networks | eye movements | gaze reinstatement | semantization | conceptual space,Memory | Naturalistic encoding/decoding | Vision,"Rebekka Heinen, Nikolai Axmacher, Ruhr University Bochum, Germany","Both semantization processes during encoding and gaze reinstatement have been linked to memory performance. Here, we tested whether these effects are reflected via similar or different representational formats and transformations at different stages of memory processing. Participants performed a visual and semantic rating task during encoding and a subsequent recognition memory task. We find that task-relevant features, especially those in a conceptual format, enhanced memory performance. Gaze similarities during encoding and retrieval were related to recognition memory performance as well, supported mainly by perceptual format. During retrieval we found higher gaze reinstatement boosting memory performance for old images and new exemplars. Interestingly, high gaze reinstatement during presentation of semantically similar concepts at retrieval impaired their correct rejection performance suggesting semantization or top-down knowledge involvement. In a next step, we will combine DNN and gaze similarities to further analyze retrieval formats.",8.32779598236084,5.709510803222656,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper4576.html?PaperNum=1482,
1393,2023,General mechanisms of task engagement in the primate frontal cortex,Jan Grohn,decision-making | engagement | motivation,Decision-making and metacognition | Attention & cognitive control / executive function,"Jan Grohn, Nima Khalighinejad, Caroline Jahn, Alessandro Bongioanni, Urs Schuffelgen, Jerome Sallet, Matthew Rushworth, Nils Kolling, Oxford, United Kingdom","Staying engaged with a task is necessary to maintain goal-directed behaviors. Although engagement varies with the specific task at hand it also exhibits continuous, intrinsic fluctuations widely. This intrinsic component of engagement is difficult to isolate behaviorally or neurally in controlled experiments with humans. By contrast, animals spontaneously move between periods of complete task engagement and disengagement, even in experimental settings. We, therefore, looked at behavior in macaques in a series of four tasks while recording fMRI signals. We identified consistent patterns of behavior predictive of impending task disengagement. This made it possible to build models capturing task-independent engagement. We identified common patterns of neural activity linked to impending task disengagement in mid-cingulate gyrus. By contrast, activity centered in perigenual anterior cingulate cortex (pgACC) was associated with maintenance of task performance. Importantly, we were able to carefully control for task-specific factors such as the reward history, choice value, and other motivational effects, such as response vigor, as indexed by response time, when identifying neural activity associated with task engagement. Moreover, we showed pgACC activity had a causal link to task engagement; transcranial ultrasound stimulation of pgACC, but not of control regions, changed task engagement/disengagement patterns.",3.3385539054870605,5.021564960479736,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper1a34.html?PaperNum=1393,
1509,2023,Generalization and Discrimination in Reinforcement Learning: Developmental Trajectories and the Potential Link with Psychotic Symptoms,Wei Chen,generalization | discrimination | semantic hierarchy | psychotic symptoms | cognitive development,"Methods, theory & everything else","Wei Chen, Aaron Nakamura, The University of Tokyo, Japan; Jialing Ding, KU Leuven, Belgium; Naohiro Okada, Shinsuke Koike, Sho Yagishita, Shin Ishii, Haruo Kasai, Yuko Yotsumoto, Ming Bo Cai, The University of Tokyo, Japan","Phasic changes in dopamine release encode reward prediction errors (RPE), which are crucial for learning stimulus-value associations. Generalizing learned associations to semantically similar stimuli allows the brain to learn efficiently, but we also need to discriminate the stimuli whose values violate the prediction based on semantic similarity. Previous evidence suggests that dopamine D1 and D2 receptors mediate the generalization and discrimination processes, respectively, while D2 receptor is a common target of antipsychotic medicine. It is unclear how the two processes develop throughout life as our knowledge matures and whether their imbalance is potentially related to psychotic symptoms. To answer these questions , we designed a reinforcement learning task in which values of stimuli follow a hierarchical structure based on semantic categories. Two versions of the task are conducted, one on online participants who also responded to questionnaires assessing psychotic symptoms and the other on age-diverse participants at a museum. Our results indicate that discrimination in the early learning phase is positively correlated with psychotic symptoms, while the recruitment of discrimination over generalization also increases over age , suggesting a potential link between abnormality in the developmental process of reinforcement learning and the risk of schizophrenia.",3.1731114387512207,5.656638145446777,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper97f5.html?PaperNum=1509,
1410,2023,Generalization in an Evidence Accumulation Task,Quan Do,,Decision-making and metacognition,"Quan Do, Michael Hasselmo, Benjamin Scott, Boston University, United States","Humans are capable of generalizing using the similarity between the abstract features of external stimuli. The neural mechanisms underlying generalization using abstract representations are unclear, and future discoveries would be facilitated by studies in animal models. Here we developed a rat model of generalization by leveraging an existing pulse-based evidence accumulation task, with the abstract rule of choosing the side with the greater number of light pulses. We trained rats (n=14) on different curricula that limit the stimulus-action associations presented during learning and then used test trials to evaluate generalization to novel stimuli. The performance of rats was consistent with a hybrid Exemplar-to-Prototype model of learning. In this model, sensory evidence in the training trials is altered by internal noise providing an expanded perceptual experience to guide generalization. Overall, our study suggests that pulse-based accumulation tasks may be used to study the neural mechanisms of generalization in animals and highlights how noise may support cognition.",3.301788330078125,3.3749794960021973,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper66ff.html?PaperNum=1410,
1459,2023,Generalization of Covariance Structure in Human and Neural Network,Zilu Liang,generalisation | abstraction | representational geometry,"Methods, theory & everything else | Neural population geometry & dynamics","Zilu Liang, Miriam Klein-Flugge, Christopher Summerfield, University of Oxford, United Kingdom","The ability to identify structure in sensory data and flexibly reuse this knowledge in new settings is key to human cognition. Here, we report neural network simulations that studied the nature of the neural representations that support transfer learning. We designed a paradigm in which agents learn the mapping from stimuli onto one label set, and tested whether they detected the covariance structure among feature dimensions (e.g. colour or shape) and generalise to 1) new stimuli from unseen combinations of features, 2) an entirely new set of labels. We train a multilayer perceptron (MLP) with one hidden layer on the same task. We derive a model of representation that supports structure transfer in our task, allowing us to make predictions for a future human study.",4.491348743438721,7.27810525894165,https://2023.ccneuro.org/view_paper26e7.html?PaperNum=1459,
